---
import DemoLessonLayout from "../../../layouts/DemoLessonLayout.astro";
const createFile =
  'python3 -c \'from pathlib import Path; Path("hello.txt").write_bytes(b"HELLO")\'';
---

<DemoLessonLayout
  title="Connect two laptops on a hotspot"
  description="Use the same phone hotspot for both laptops and find the server by its assigned IPv4 address."
  downloads
  next={{ path: "tutorials/tcp-demo/docker/", label: "Try Docker" }}
>
  <section class="lab-section" aria-labelledby="hotspot-docker">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <h2 id="hotspot-docker" tabindex="-1">
        Set up both laptops
      </h2>
      <p>Use Python 3 and the lab scripts in a fresh folder on each laptop.</p>
    </div>
    <h3>Two laptops on one phone hotspot</h3>
    <ol class="lab-steps">
      <li>
        Join both laptops to the <strong>same phone hotspot</strong>. Save the
        scripts on their respective laptops before the rehearsal. Use a
        synthetic practice file such as HELLO.
      </li>
      <li>
        Find the server <em>laptop’s</em> IPv4 address in its Wi-Fi settings,
        then replace the placeholder below in both commands. Use the laptop’s
        address on the hotspot rather than the phone’s gateway address.
      </li>
      <li>
        On the server laptop, allow the connection on this network if the local
        firewall prompts you. Start the server and wait for its waiting message.
        <pre class="lab-code"><code>python3 server.py --host SERVER_LAPTOP_IP</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        On the client laptop, create the sample and send it.
        <pre class="lab-code"><code>{createFile + "\npython3 client.py hello.txt --host SERVER_LAPTOP_IP"}</code></pre>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Rehearse with the actual phone and laptops. The file crosses your local
      hotspot network. If the laptops cannot reach each other, recheck the
      server’s assigned IP and use the one-laptop demo if needed.
    </p>
    <details>
      <summary>What to check if the client cannot connect</summary>
      <ul class="lab-steps">
        <li>
          <strong>No waiting message.</strong> Check the server IP against the
          address of the Wi‑Fi interface. A hotspot can assign a different
          address when you reconnect.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Connection refused.</strong> Start the server first and check
          that its terminal says it is waiting on port 5001. It exits after each
          transfer.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Connection times out.</strong> Check both devices are on the
          same hotspot and are allowed to reach each other. Check the server's
          firewall for that network. If the hotspot isolates devices, rehearse
          on one laptop.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </details>
    <p>
      <strong>Check your work.</strong> The client prints OK and the copy
      appears on the server laptop. Rename the copy before the next rehearsal.
    </p>
  </section>
</DemoLessonLayout>
